WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
Never let it collapse.

2
00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.760
Three passes
at an unsupervised cat's cradle.

3
00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.360
So opening the pass.

4
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.680
So beginning with my hands, there's
a figure I've carried for nearly 30 years,

5
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.960
borrowed from Alcaraz and Stone,
who borrowed it from Donna Haraway.

6
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.760
The child's Game of Cat's Cradle
a loop of string, two pairs of hands,

7
00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:20.640
configurations passed back
and forth, turned, held in productive

8
00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:24.080
tension, rather than allowed to collapse
into a vocal account.

9
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.800
Stone reminds us
that we pass these accounts from hand

10
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.360
to hand within webs of power
that distort them,

11
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.400
and that the important thing
about a cat's cradle.

12
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.400
The only rule, really,
is that you can never let it collapse.

13
00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:36.400
What I'm going to do
today is pass something,

14
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.600
a configuration I've been holding
in various forms for 25 years,

15
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.120
and I'm going to try to hand it to you
without dropping it.

16
00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.480
And I'm curious about what shape

17
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.480
it takes in your hands,
because that's how the game works.

18
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.760
It changes as it moves,
and that isn't a failure of fidelity.

19
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.320
That's the whole point.

20
00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.200
I should tell you something else
before I begin,

21
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.760
and it kind of fits with this panel.

22
00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:58.560
In the spirit of showing my weaving,
I initially imagine this talk with AI,

23
00:00:58.600 --> 00:00:59.840
a rival pattern maker.

24
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.600
I asked it to read 25 years
of my public documents

25
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.880
and find patterns I could no longer see,
and it did find them.

26
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.840
It can hold things at once in a way that
I can't, and notice that a single metaphor

27
00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.960
has been quietly doing structural work,
of course, across a quarter century.

28
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.200
My thinking it's

29
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.240
a remarkable weaver in its way,
but it can't summon the feeling

30
00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:20.360
of the defense room.

31
00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:24.000
It doesn't know how hands at a keyboard
work as an embodied practice.

32
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.560
It doesn't know, taken as touch,
and it's never lost.

33
00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.680
An interlocutor who was also a friend.

34
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:29.120
It can't

35
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.040
know that
the best transmission of knowledge,

36
00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.960
the best supervision,
is so often about love.

37
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.240
So here we go past one the wild space.

38
00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.040
In the late 1990s,
I was a doctoral student

39
00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.440
making something
the institution didn't have a name for,

40
00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.320
which is a polite way of saying
making something

41
00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:47.320
the institution had names for,
and most of them were awful.

42
00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:51.080
The work became Building Feminist Theory,
a hypertext heuristics

43
00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:54.400
native hypermedia dissertation, meaning
it could not exist in print,

44
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.600
could not be translated without remainder.

45
00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.600
It was composed for the screen and
for the associate of logic of the link.

46
00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:05.000
It was Canada's first born digital
dissertation, and nobody knew that yet.

47
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.480
Nobody was keeping track.
And that's part of the story.

48
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.120
Long before I met hypertext software,
I wrote hypertext.

49
00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.040
Really, like many of you,

50
00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.280
with straws and string and handwritten
letters, cross-legged on the floor

51
00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.200
with scissors and a glue stick in an empty
room, arranging and rearranging 400

52
00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:19.280
handwritten sheets,

53
00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.360
drawing arrows between them with thick
magic markers covering an entire room.

54
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.920
And yes, it did make me a little crazy.

55
00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.160
When I finally encountered
digital hypertext, it was a homecoming,

56
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.280
and putting together hypertext writing
strategies and feminist theory

57
00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.600
seemed to me obvious and irresistible.

58
00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.000
Weaving political

59
00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:39.600
part.

60
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.800
I've not always told I was directed
not to pursue the project.

61
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:45.160
My committee fell apart unsupervised.

62
00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.240
People didn't get it.

63
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.800
I made mobiles and tactile visualizations,
trying to meet people's literacies

64
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:50.440
where they were.

65
00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:55.040
I was more than once to print everything
out, and I was racing against regulation.

66
00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.320
Even as I prepared to defend,

67
00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:57.960
the university
was circulating a discussion

68
00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:00.120
paper proposing
that all electronic dissertations

69
00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.800
be submitted in 12 point times
Roman, with a 1.5in margin.

70
00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.600
The future of writing is PDF.

71
00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.680
The unsupervised space was closing
even as I worked inside it.

72
00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.600
So when we talk about unregulated,
unsupervised spaces,

73
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.120
I want to name
the texture of that freedom.

74
00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:15.040
It was real.

75
00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:17.400
No frameworks, no precedent, no criteria.

76
00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.760
Genuinely wild,
I think. Ripe for invention.

77
00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.880
A space where a graduate student
could decide that a linking structure

78
00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:25.360
might constitute
a dissertations intellectual core,

79
00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.440
and there was no rule
that said otherwise.

80
00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:28.880
But it was also dangerous.

81
00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.120
No protection, no legibility,
no guarantee that the work would count.

82
00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.280
Working in the shadows of an institution
that wished you stop.

83
00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.040
Desire and danger are not opposites
in a space like that.

84
00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:44.880
It took me a long time to find
a supervisor and then I found Yoan Davies.

85
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.440
Yoan looked at something genuinely
strange and said, this is worth making.

86
00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:51.840
The supervision
he offered wasn't approval.

87
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.720
It was the willingness
to hold the other end of the string,

88
00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:57.000
to stand between a strange new thing
and an institution that couldn't yet

89
00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.000
read it, translating in both directions,
vouching, absorbing

90
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.120
some of the institutional weather
so that I could keep my hands free.

91
00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:06.280
The best supervisory relationships
are transmissions

92
00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:09.680
of method of nerve,
of the permission to invent and unread

93
00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:11.280
space.

94
00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.760
And when
they work, they're also about love,

95
00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:15.560
the love of a mind
that takes your thinking,

96
00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:16.960
making seriously enough

97
00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:21.360
to push it further than you believed
it could go with the other end held.

98
00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.800
I built over 1400
Lexia more than 17,000 links,

99
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:26.960
and the claim I staked to everything
I was this.

100
00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.840
The linking structure
was the epistemological intervention,

101
00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:32.880
not the nodes, the constellation,
not the cloth, the weaving,

102
00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:34.560
a thought sculpture, my library,

103
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.920
my orientation, my contradictions
delivered whole the all at once.

104
00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:41.880
And as of theory as I built it, the
scaffolding of the academic enterprise,

105
00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:46.800
the unconscious of the philosophical line,
made, visible, coded, saved and offered.

106
00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:50.280
The making was sometimes lonely,
but the making was also full of company.

107
00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.560
The dissertation became a system
filled with small gifts

108
00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:55.960
from people who encountered it
on its way to completion.

109
00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.080
Friends laughing into my microphone
so I could have

110
00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.080
audio files, films from an MRI

111
00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:00.720
so I could have

112
00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:04.400
images of spines and organs, baby
pictures, pockets turned inside out

113
00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:08.840
to reveal mysterious things from a digital
microscope, scans, scraps of quilting,

114
00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.720
a braid cut off and saved in tissue

115
00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.800
petals kept for years between the pages
of a favorite book, hand to the scanner.

116
00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:16.320
The pages were self-portraits

117
00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.360
and portraits of a community
that assembled itself around

118
00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.600
the making even before
a community of readers existed.

119
00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:24.640
In keeping with the collaborative,
collective memory

120
00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:27.720
making spirit of the feminist hypertext
that inspired it,

121
00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:31.440
the work was woven out of relation,
that is, also love.

122
00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:35.280
When the work began to circulate,
I discovered that my readers more often

123
00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:38.280
than not read nodes
and not links reluctant prequels.

124
00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:41.720
They read words and quotations
as the content and the structure itself

125
00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:44.840
my actual intervention,
its contours, its conventions,

126
00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:49.120
the new ground I'd hoped to break remained
largely unintelligible,

127
00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:52.600
and I compounded the problem myself,
translating the work from story space

128
00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:55.280
to HTML at the last minute,
for ease of circulation,

129
00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:57.880
I flattened the thought
sculpture into a linear catalog.

130
00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:00.960
The spatial cues
vanished, the arrows became menu bars.

131
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.440
In terms, I aligned my own work
with the assembly line.

132
00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:07.840
A struggle with interface is a struggle
with meaning, and I built something

133
00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:10.840
for for literacies that did not yet exist.

134
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.080
Past two the unsupervised defense

135
00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:18.480
John Davies died snorkeling in Cuba
just weeks before my defense.

136
00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:20.800
For a long time,
I couldn't just say that plainly.

137
00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:26.000
It was my supervisor, and he was also a
cherished friend and a spectacular mentor.

138
00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:29.360
The grief of losing
an interlocutor is a specific grief.

139
00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:31.440
It takes not only the person,
but the particular self

140
00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.520
you were in conversation with them, the
thinking that only existed between you.

141
00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:38.960
Some links, when they break,
take down whole constellations

142
00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.160
and I defend it anyway, unsupervised
now in the most literal sense.

143
00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:46.640
So think about what a doctoral defense is,
what it does institutionally.

144
00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:48.440
It's the moment of reproduction.

145
00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:51.520
The university looks at a body of work
and the person who made it and says,

146
00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:55.480
this counts as knowledge, and this person
is now authorized to make more of it.

147
00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:57.400
It's the mechanism by which institutions

148
00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:01.280
perpetuate themselves, their standards,
their sense of what thinking looks like.

149
00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:04.800
The supervisor is the hinge
of that mechanism, the one who spent years

150
00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:07.880
vouching, translating
between the candidate and the institution,

151
00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:11.000
making the strange thing legible,
especially when the work

152
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:14.320
is trying to refuse
the very terms of legibility on offer.

153
00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:17.440
I walked into that room without him
carrying a work that argued

154
00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:20.120
for a new kind of knowledge
making into an institution

155
00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:22.360
that had already told me
it found the project difficult

156
00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:25.120
without the person
who'd been building the bridge between us.

157
00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:25.840
At precisely

158
00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:29.840
the moment, the institution performs
its most concentrated, active judgment.

159
00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:32.480
But the cat's cradle did not collapse.

160
00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:35.600
Maybe the work had enough
internal coherence to hold itself.

161
00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:37.880
Maybe the committee
found their own ways in.

162
00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:41.440
But I also believe the faith
Joan had transmitted was so thorough

163
00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:44.400
that I carried it into the room
like a second pair of hands.

164
00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.920
Transmission survives the transmitter.

165
00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:49.640
That may be the most important thing
I know about mentorship.

166
00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:52.600
What a great supervisor gives
you is not supervision.

167
00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:55.160
It's the internalized voice
that says, keep going.

168
00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:58.360
This is worth making and keep saying it
even after.

169
00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:01.320
So here's the theoretical claim
I want to make from inside

170
00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:02.400
the personal one.

171
00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:05.160
Unsupervised spaces are never simply free.

172
00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:08.440
There's spaces where invention
and exposure arrive together,

173
00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:11.640
where the cost of intelligibility is real,
where the absence

174
00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.960
of an interlocutors felt is grief
and not mere inconvenience.

175
00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.560
The wild space gives you room
to build structures

176
00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:21.120
no one has imagined, but it also leaves
you profoundly alone with them.

177
00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:24.920
Both things are true, and we falsify
the history of experimental work,

178
00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:28.760
all the marginal shadowed on archived work
this field is built on.

179
00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:32.240
When we romanticize the freedom
without accounting for the exposure.

180
00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:35.320
Alterity is generative and expensive.

181
00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:39.600
Someone pays for the new form,
usually in isolation, usually in advance.

182
00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:41.560
Past three.

183
00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:47.440
25. A different kind of unsupervised.

184
00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:53.440
Large language models traverse archives
at scales I could not have imagined.

185
00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:57.920
When 1400 lexis felt like the world
they produced fluent, plausible,

186
00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:00.720
extensively connected outputs,
and they are, in a meaningful sense,

187
00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:03.760
unsupervised,
trained past the horizon of meaningful

188
00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:07.760
oversight, deployed into institutions
busily withdrawing their own supervisory

189
00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:10.760
structures, producing connections
whose logic is opaque

190
00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:12.520
even to the people who build them.

191
00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:15.600
I want to bring my 25 year old story
to bear on this moment.

192
00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:17.560
Accountability.

193
00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:20.440
My dissertation claimed
the linking was the thinking.

194
00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:22.560
The 17,000 links
were not connective tissue.

195
00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:26.200
They were arguments made by
a situated intelligence showing her work.

196
00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:27.960
Me a link was a claim.

197
00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:29.800
These two things belong in conversation.

198
00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:32.880
And I'm the one saying
that you could trace my constellation.

199
00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:35.600
You could interrogate it. Why this link?
Why are you asserting?

200
00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:38.960
What are you asserting by this adjacency
and hold me to answers?

201
00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:41.840
In some ways,
I feel it was actually me at that time.

202
00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:44.840
The shape of six years of thinking
and knowing.

203
00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:47.920
The links were coded,
saved in memory beyond my own weighting.

204
00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:51.440
My readers in 2000 read nodes,
and I understood that as a failure

205
00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:54.600
of interface, a problem awaiting
better tools and better literacies.

206
00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:55.960
And I was partly right.

207
00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:58.960
No one needs help following a link.

208
00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:02.120
But what we face
today is not a failure interface.

209
00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:05.320
Rather than the Constitution,

210
00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:10.160
the intelligence unlockable,
there's no weaver to call to account.

211
00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:14.440
We get nodes fluent, voluminous
without weaving, structures never neutral.

212
00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:16.800
The link was always
where the politics lived.

213
00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:17.680
So what does it mean

214
00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:20.880
to fill the world with connections
no one can be asked to defend?

215
00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:24.400
But even if we solved accountability,
even if every model output arrived

216
00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.840
with its constellation
beautifully diagramed,

217
00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:28.280
something else would still be missing.

218
00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:31.640
And it's the thing I've been circling
the eros of linking,

219
00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:34.920
because the deepest claim
of that quarter century ago dissertation

220
00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:39.280
was never that links carry arguments
or not just that it was

221
00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:42.480
that making the links
remakes the maker hands at the keyboard,

222
00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:45.240
typing as an embodied practice,
the body and the loop of thought.

223
00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:47.960
17,000 times
I asked what belongs with what?

224
00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:50.280
What do I desire to place beside
what and happen?

225
00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:52.040
What happens to me when I do that?

226
00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:56.320
The process gave me an insider's intimacy
with negative dialectics and alchemy,

227
00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:58.240
in which the theory
and the theorist are transformed

228
00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:00.400
together, tools
and methods that change us.

229
00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:04.080
And this is what I meant then
and now by hypertext as an epistemology

230
00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:06.720
intervention into the Academy.

231
00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:09.760
Not a delivery format for knowledge,
but a practice of knowing,

232
00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:13.160
desiring, associative,
embodied, and transformative.

233
00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:16.800
The weaving of memory,
the Penelope work of recollection.

234
00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:20.880
You can't outsource the Penelope work
not because the machine does it badly,

235
00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:22.960
but because the doing was never
the product.

236
00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:26.000
The doing was the point,
the doing was the knowing.

237
00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:29.360
So when we delegate the linking,
the association, the pattern

238
00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:32.760
finding, the traversal of the archive,
I'm not just interested in

239
00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:35.240
who or what is accountable
for the connections.

240
00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:38.760
I'm only asking what happens to us
when the thinking no longer passes

241
00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:39.840
through our hands?

242
00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:41.840
What ways of knowing quietly disappear

243
00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:44.160
when the constellation arrives
pre-assembled?

244
00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:48.200
What happens to Eros, to the desire
that pulls two texts together?

245
00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:50.600
The charge of the unexpected adjacency?

246
00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:54.640
The early hypertext theory
was not embarrassed to name.

247
00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:57.640
When the connection costs
nothing and changes no one.

248
00:11:58.040 --> 00:11:58.600
I say

249
00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:02.360
all this as someone who first imagined
this very talk with a rival pattern maker.

250
00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:04.720
I've never been one to perform purity.

251
00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:07.600
The machine saw patterns in my archive
I could no longer see.

252
00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:10.960
I supplied what it couldn't
grief, hands, room, love.

253
00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:14.520
Perhaps the division of labor
is one livable future, but it's livable

254
00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:17.760
only if we stay awake to
what must not be delegated.

255
00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:21.640
And that is the unsupervised space where
and right now, like the one I worked

256
00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:25.440
in 25 years ago, is the space
where these defaults are being set.

257
00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:29.920
Without frameworks, without precedents,
the regulations are always about to land.

258
00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:33.040
The question is what we will have built
and become before they do.

259
00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:36.960
One last thing about shadows and archives
building feminist theory

260
00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:37.960
was never archived.

261
00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:40.600
The institution that examined
it didn't know how to keep it.

262
00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:44.480
The story space data sets had been erasing
themselves from my disks for years.

263
00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:47.000
Canada's
first born digital dissertation survives

264
00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:49.680
now mainly as screenshots, memory,
and the thinking.

265
00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:53.440
It's set in motion in one person
over a very long time, and the way it

266
00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:55.600
ricochets into all of my poems.

267
00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:59.200
So this talk may be the work's
primary transmission, this zoom.

268
00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:02.400
Right now, it's
recording the closest thing to an archive.

269
00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:06.040
I've been haunted for years by a version
I might make a fully immersive

270
00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:09.200
constellation
where 1400 nodes hang like stars, sewn

271
00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:12.200
together with virtual string,
ready for a walk through.

272
00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:16.000
Maybe I still will make that the cat's
cradle can never be allowed to collapse.

273
00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:19.080
Cat's cradle is a theory of transmission.

274
00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:21.280
It's not about the string,
it's about the passing.

275
00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:23.440
And the passing requires
another pair of hands.

276
00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:24.600
It can't be done alone.

277
00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:26.480
That's the figures. Quiet insistence.

278
00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:29.280
And it's turned out to be the through line
of everything I've told you.

279
00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:32.240
The friends whose laughter and braids
and petals are woven into the work.

280
00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:35.680
The supervisor who held the other end
of something genuinely strange.

281
00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:38.360
The committee
that caught what he could no longer pass.

282
00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:42.040
The readers who over decades
learned to read links the rival pattern

283
00:13:42.040 --> 00:13:45.200
maker that helped me see my own archive
and now you.

284
00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:50.320
But I passed you as a constellation with
three points a wild, unsupervised space

285
00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:54.720
where new form of knowledge was possible
and dangerous and made in love.

286
00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:57.720
A defense room where the institutional
mechanism of reproduction met.

287
00:13:57.720 --> 00:13:59.440
A worker could not read without the person

288
00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:02.480
who had been translating,
and where the string impossibly held.

289
00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:06.360
And this moment where we're all inside
unsupervised systems whose weaving

290
00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:10.240
we cannot see, deciding mostly by default,
mostly without noticing

291
00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:12.680
which parts of thinking
will keep in our hands.

292
00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:15.120
The question
these three points holds intention.

293
00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:17.000
What are the ethics of linking?

294
00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:19.280
Who answers for the connections
being made?

295
00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:22.760
And what do we lose in eros, in alchemy,
in the ways of knowing that

296
00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:26.320
live in the making when the weaving leads
our leaves our hands?

297
00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:29.400
The important thing about a cat's cradle
is that you can never let

298
00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:32.400
it collapse your hands now.

299
00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:33.160
Thank you.
